[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Shadow DOM: Encapsulating Styles and Markup within Web Components"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Shadow DOM is a crucial feature of Web Components that allows you to encapsulate the internal structure, style, and behavior of a component, keeping it separate from the rest of the document. This encapsulation prevents styles and scripts from the main document from accidentally affecting the component, and vice versa. It's like having a mini-document inside your main document."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use Shadow DOM?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Encapsulation: Styles and scripts are scoped to the shadow DOM, preventing conflicts with the main document or other components."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reusability: Create self-contained components that can be easily reused across different projects without worrying about style or script collisions."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Simplified Development: Easier to reason about and maintain component code because it's isolated."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Shadow DOM"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a shadow DOM, you first need a host element. This is a regular HTML element to which you'll attach the shadow DOM. You then use the `attachShadow()` method to create the shadow DOM and attach it to the host."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"const host = document.querySelector('#my-element');
const shadow = host.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });

shadow.innerHTML = `<p>This is inside the shadow DOM!</p>`;"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's break down this code:"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`document.querySelector('#my-element')`: Selects the host element with the ID `my-element`."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`host.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' })`: Attaches a shadow DOM to the host element. The `mode` option can be either `open` or `closed`.  `open` means you can access the shadow DOM from JavaScript outside the component using `host.shadowRoot`. `closed` prevents external access."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`shadow.innerHTML = ...`: Sets the content of the shadow DOM.  Here, we're adding a simple paragraph."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Complete Example"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Shadow DOM Example</title>
  <style>
    p {
      color: blue; /* Style for the main document */
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="my-element"></div>

  <script>
    const host = document.querySelector('#my-element');
    const shadow = host.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });

    shadow.innerHTML = `
      <style>
        p {
          color: red; /* Style for the shadow DOM */
        }
      </style>
      <p>This is inside the shadow DOM!</p>
      <p>This is also inside the shadow DOM!</p>
    `;

    const paragraph = document.createElement('p');
    paragraph.textContent = 'This is outside the shadow DOM!';
    document.body.appendChild(paragraph);
  </script>
</body>
</html>"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, you'll notice that the paragraph inside the shadow DOM is red, while the paragraph outside is blue. This demonstrates the style encapsulation provided by the shadow DOM."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Shadow DOM Modes: 'open' vs. 'closed'"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `mode` option in `attachShadow()` controls whether the shadow DOM is accessible from JavaScript outside the component."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`open`: If the mode is `open`, you can access the shadow root using the `shadowRoot` property of the host element (e.g., `host.shadowRoot`)."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`closed`: If the mode is `closed`, the `shadowRoot` property returns `null`, preventing external access to the shadow DOM.  This provides stronger encapsulation."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"// Open mode
const hostOpen = document.querySelector('#open-element');
const shadowOpen = hostOpen.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
console.log(hostOpen.shadowRoot); // Returns the ShadowRoot object

// Closed mode
const hostClosed = document.querySelector('#closed-element');
const shadowClosed = hostClosed.attachShadow({ mode: 'closed' });
console.log(hostClosed.shadowRoot); // Returns null"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choose the mode that best suits your needs. `open` is generally preferred for easier debugging and testing, while `closed` offers stronger encapsulation when necessary."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Styling Shadow DOM Content"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can style the content within the shadow DOM using standard CSS. Styles defined inside the shadow DOM are scoped to that DOM and do not affect the rest of the document. Similarly, styles from the main document generally do not affect the shadow DOM, with a few exceptions (e.g., inherited styles like `color` and custom properties)."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"const host = document.querySelector('#styled-element');
const shadow = host.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });

shadow.innerHTML = `
  <style>
    p {
      color: green;
      font-size: 18px;
    }
  </style>
  <p>This paragraph is styled within the shadow DOM.</p>
`;"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates how to style a paragraph element within the shadow DOM. The styles are encapsulated and will only apply to elements within that shadow DOM."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Using CSS Custom Properties (Variables)"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"CSS custom properties (also known as CSS variables) can be used to pass styling information from the main document into the shadow DOM. This allows for some degree of customization of the component's appearance from the outside."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":":root {
  --main-color: blue;
}

#custom-element {
  --shadow-color: orange;
}

const host = document.querySelector('#custom-element');
const shadow = host.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });

shadow.innerHTML = `
  <style>
    p {
      color: var(--shadow-color, var(--main-color));
    }
  </style>
  <p>This paragraph uses a CSS custom property.</p>
`;"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the paragraph's color is determined by the `--shadow-color` custom property. If `--shadow-color` is not defined on the host element (or any of its ancestors), it falls back to the `--main-color` defined on the `:root` element. If neither is defined, the paragraph will have its default color."},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Event Retargeting"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Shadow DOM also affects event handling. When an event originates within the shadow DOM, the event's `target` property is adjusted to maintain encapsulation.  The event is *retargeted* as it crosses the shadow boundary. This means that from the perspective of the main document, the event appears to originate from the host element, not from the specific element within the shadow DOM."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"<div id="event-host">
  <!-- Shadow DOM will be attached here -->
</div>

<script>
  const host = document.getElementById('event-host');
  const shadow = host.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });

  shadow.innerHTML = '<button id="shadow-button">Click me</button>';

  host.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
    console.log('Event target:', event.target);
  });
</script>"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, even though the click event originates from the button inside the shadow DOM, the event target reported in the main document's event listener will be the `event-host` div, not the button itself. This prevents the outside world from directly interacting with the internal elements of the shadow DOM through events."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Slots: Distributing Content into the Shadow DOM"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"Slots provide a mechanism for injecting content from the light DOM (the main document) into specific locations within the shadow DOM. This allows you to create reusable components with customizable content."},{"order":37,"type":"code","text":"<template id="my-template">
  <style>
    ::slotted(p) {
      color: purple;
    }
  </style>
  <slot name="my-paragraph"></slot>
  <slot></slot>
</template>

<custom-element>
  <p slot="my-paragraph">This is a slotted paragraph.</p>
  <p>This is another paragraph that will go into the default slot.</p>
</custom-element>

<script>
  class CustomElement extends HTMLElement {
    constructor() {
      super();
      this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
      const template = document.getElementById('my-template');
      this.shadowRoot.appendChild(template.content.cloneNode(true));
    }
  }
  customElements.define('custom-element', CustomElement);
</script>"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here's how slots work:"},{"order":39,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`<slot>`:  A placeholder in the shadow DOM where content from the light DOM will be inserted."},{"order":40,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`name`:  Slots can have a `name` attribute.  Content in the light DOM with a matching `slot` attribute will be inserted into that named slot."},{"order":41,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default Slot: If a slot doesn't have a name, it's the default slot. Content in the light DOM without a `slot` attribute will be inserted into the default slot."},{"order":42,"type":"bullet_point","text":"::slotted(): This CSS pseudo-element allows you to style elements that are slotted into the shadow DOM."},{"order":43,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":44,"type":"paragraph","text":"Shadow DOM is a powerful tool for building encapsulated and reusable web components. By understanding how to create shadow DOMs, style their content, handle events, and use slots, you can create robust and maintainable components that enhance your web applications."}]
